title: Schema endpoints
tags: Schema
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: GET the loaded schema, reload it from disk, and check the data against it.

|!Endpoint |!Does |
|<<cmd "GET /repos/:repo/schema">> |the schema as loaded (the file's structure), or `{"version": 1, "groups": []}` without one — what a client reads to build forms and templates |
|<<cmd "POST /repos/:repo/schema/reload">> |re-reads the file and replaces the schema at once; answers the new schema, or `400` naming the offending constraint, the old schema staying in effect |
|<<cmd "POST /repos/:repo/schema/check">> |lists every violation, optionally among the metarecords a query matches |

`check` takes an optional `{"query": <Query>, "limit": 20}`. `limit` caps the
violations returned: the scan stops once it is passed — a repository with hundreds
of thousands of violations never builds an unusable answer — and the answer then
says `truncated: true`, `checked` counting the metarecords actually examined.

```
{"checked": 1542, "truncated": false,
 "violations": [
   {"metarecord_uuid": "abc…", "type": "film", "field": "rating", "kind": "type",
    "message": "value of type string not allowed (expected: int)"},
   {"metarecord_uuid": "abc…", "type": null, "field": "rating", "kind": "max_cardinality",
    "message": "3 rows, maximum is 1"}]}
```

`type` is the constraint's origin — a type name, or `null` for a global constraint;
`kind` is `type`, `min_cardinality` or `max_cardinality`. The same items are what a
refused write carries. `check` writes nothing, but holds the repository while it
scans: writes wait for it.
